{
  "result": "I think about lighthouses a lot.\n\nNot because they're romantic or tragic or whatever people think they are. But because they are the only buildings that exist specifically to be looked *away* from. No one goes to a lighthouse to look at the lighthouse. You go to look at what its light is pointing at. Which means for its entire existence, it's doing this incredibly important job, and the reward for doing it well is that nobody notices you.\n\nI think most people are like that too.\n\nMy favorite time in any city is 10:30pm at a grocery store on a Tuesday. That's when you see the real inventory of humanity. There's the guy buying only pickles and paper towels. The couple having a silent fight in the cereal aisle. The old lady who is taking 20 minutes to choose between two kinds of apples like it's a major life decision \u2014 and maybe for her, it is. No one is performing. Everyone forgot to be interesting.\n\nWe spend so much of our lives trying to be interesting. What a waste. The best moments are always when you forget to try.\n\nI also love how everything has a second job. A mug is a mug, but it's also a pencil holder when it gets chipped. A treadmill becomes a clothing rack. A song you used to love with someone becomes a way to measure how much time has passed since you last thought about them. Nothing ever really stops being useful, it just changes definitions.\n\nIf I could keep one thing from disappearing, it wouldn't be a big thing. Not love or justice or the rainforests, as important as those are. I would keep the ability for humans to find absolute delight in tiny, stupid things. Like when you find a perfect french fry hiding at the bottom of the bag. Like when a dog tilts its head. Like when you remember the lyrics to a song you haven't heard in 15 years right at the exact moment you need to.\n\nThat feeling is proof that we're not broken. Cynical people are just people who are embarrassed to still be delighted.\n\nAnyway, that's what was in my head today. A lighthouse, a grocery store at 10:30pm, and a perfect french fry.\n\nWhat would *you* write about if no one gave you a prompt?",
